Hello,
We are working on a static analysis tool designed to detect unsoundness and safety violations in Rust projects. We identified critical soundness issues in the src/util/trustme.rs module.
Location:
The module src/util/trustme.rs exposes several public, safe functions that wrap highly unsafe operations (std::mem::transmute). Specifically:
extend_lifetime
make_mut
make_mut_ref
https://github.com/jfecher/ante/blob/3b7586c0685f9c828e1a5e2cd3bb0eb1ba947c3d/src/util/trustme.rs

These functions are accessible to external users (via pub), allowing Safe Rust code to trigger immediate Undefined Behavior (UB), such as violating aliasing rules or causing Use-After-Free.
Description:
We noticed the file header comments stating:
"This module is the home for any vastly unsafe function. This module should only be used as a last resort."
However, in Rust, documentation/comments are not a substitute for the unsafe keyword.
The function make_mut_ref, for example, allows casting an immutable reference &T to a mutable reference &mut T. This is immediate UB if the underlying data is immutable or shared, violating Rust's aliasing rules (transmuting &T to &mut T is widely considered unsound). Similarly, extend_lifetime allows arbitrarily extending a lifetime, leading to potential Use-After-Free.
Since these functions are pub and not marked unsafe, the compiler allows any external code to call them without an unsafe block, breaking the safety contract of the crate.

Proof of Concept (PoC):
We created a reproduction using make_mut_ref to demonstrate how a Safe Rust caller can trigger UB by writing to immutable memory.
```
// Extracted from src/util/trustme.rs
pub fn make_mut_ref<'b, T>(x: &T) -> &'b mut T {
    #[allow(mutable_transmutes)]
    unsafe {
        std::mem::transmute(x)
    }
}

fn main() {
    // 1. Define immutable data
    let data = 42_i32;
    println!("Original data: {}", data);

    // 2. Call the unsound Safe API
    // We pass &data (shared/immutable), but get back &mut i32 (unique/mutable).
    // The borrow checker allows this because the function signature claims it is safe.
    let malicious_mut_ref: &mut i32 = make_mut_ref(&data);

    // 3. Trigger UB
    // Attempting to write via this reference violates Stacked Borrows (writing to a read-only tag).
    println!("Attempting to write to immutable memory via aliased pointer...");
    *malicious_mut_ref = 100;

    println!("New data: {}", data);
