```
fn gen_random_array<I>(&self, shape: &[usize], dist: I) -> NdArray<T>
where
    I: Distribution<f64>,
{
    let size: usize = shape.iter().cloned().product();
    let mut rng = self.rng.borrow_mut();
    let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(size);
    unsafe {
        for i in 0..size {
            *buf.get_unchecked_mut(i) = T::from(dist.sample(&mut *rng)).unwrap();
        }
        buf.set_len(size);
        NdArray::from_shape_vec_unchecked(shape, buf)
    }
}

```
The issue occurs because:

In gen_random_array, the function calculates size as the product of all elements in the shape array: let size: usize = shape.iter().cloned().product()
When given very large values in the shape array, this product will overflow and wrap to a much smaller value in release mode
The function then creates a vector with this incorrectly small capacity: let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(size)
It writes to this buffer using get_unchecked_mut(i) and sets the length using buf.set_len(size)
Finally, it creates an ndarray with NdArray::from_shape_vec_unchecked(shape, buf)
This creates an ndarray with dimensions that don't match its actual buffer size, which will lead to out-of-bounds memory access when the array is used

The lack of overflow checking in the product calculation creates a direct path from public input to memory safety violation.

A valid path is pub fn exponential -> fn gen_random_array

